        ("According to the Empirical Rule, what percentage of data falls within 3 standard deviations of the mean?", "Approximately 99.7%", ["95%", "68%", "100%"], "Approximately 99.73% of normal distribution data lies within mu +/- 3 sigma."),
        ("What is the formula to convert a raw score x into a standard z-score?", "z = (x - mu) / sigma", ["z = (x - sigma) / mu", "z = x * sigma - mu", "z = (mu - x) / sigma^2"], "A z-score standardizes observations: z = (x - mu) / sigma."),
        ("What is the mean and standard deviation of the Standard Normal Distribution (Z)?", "Mean = 0, Standard Deviation = 1", ["Mean = 1, Standard Deviation = 0", "Mean = 1, Standard Deviation = 1", "Mean = 100, SD = 15"], "The standard normal distribution Z ~ N(0, 1) has mean zero and unit variance."),
        ("What is the value range of Pearson's correlation coefficient (r)?", "-1.0 to +1.0", ["0.0 to 1.0", "-infinity to +infinity", "0.0 to 100.0"], "Pearson's r ranges from -1 (perfect negative correlation) to +1 (perfect positive correlation)."),
        ("What does a correlation coefficient r = 0 indicate between two variables?", "No linear relationship", ["Perfect positive relationship", "Strong non-linear relationship", "Calculation error"], "An r of 0 indicates absence of any linear relationship between the variables."),
        ("In a right-skewed (positively skewed) distribution, which order of central tendency holds?", "Mean > Median > Mode", ["Mode > Median > Mean", "Mean = Median = Mode", "Median > Mean > Mode"], "Positive skew pulls the mean rightward toward the long tail: Mean > Median > Mode."),
        ("In a left-skewed (negatively skewed) distribution, which order of central tendency holds?", "Mode > Median > Mean", ["Mean > Median > Mode", "Mean = Median = Mode", "Mean > Mode > Median"], "Negative skew pulls the mean leftward into the long tail: Mode > Median > Mean."),
        ("What is the Null Hypothesis (H0) typically postulated as in hypothesis testing?", "Statement of no effect, no difference, or status quo", ["Statement that the researcher hopes to prove", "Statement that variance is infinite", "Statement of guaranteed correlation"], "The null hypothesis posits no real effect; statistical testing evaluates evidence against it."),
        ("What is the Alternative Hypothesis (H1 or Ha)?", "Statement that there is an effect or difference contrary to null", ["Statement of zero change", "Always identical to H0", "The error term"], "The alternative hypothesis represents the research claim that an effect exists."),
        ("What is a Type I Error in hypothesis testing?", "Rejecting the true null hypothesis (False Positive)", ["Failing to reject false null", "Calculating incorrect mean", "Sample size too large"], "Type I error (alpha) occurs when H0 is true but erroneously rejected."),
        ("What is a Type II Error in hypothesis testing?", "Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (False Negative)", ["Rejecting a true null", "Finding p-value < 0.05", "Dividing by zero"], "Type II error (beta) occurs when H0 is false but the test fails to reject it."),
        ("What is the significance level (alpha) conventionally set to in scientific hypothesis tests?", "0.05 (5%)", ["0.50 (50%)", "0.00 (0%)", "0.25 (25%)"], "Alpha is conventionally set to 0.05, accepting a 5% risk of committing a Type I error."),
        ("If a hypothesis test yields a p-value of 0.02 and alpha = 0.05, the decision is to:", "Reject the Null Hypothesis (statistically significant)", ["Fail to reject Null Hypothesis", "Accept Null Hypothesis as proven", "Discard the sample"], "Since p-value (0.02) <= alpha (0.05), we reject H0 in favor of the alternative hypothesis."),
        ("What does the Central Limit Theorem (CLT) state regarding sample means?", "Sampling distribution of sample mean approaches normal distribution as sample size grows", ["All populations are normal", "Standard deviation becomes zero", "Mean equals variance"], "For any population with finite variance, sample mean distribution approaches normal for n >= 30."),
        ("What is the formula for the Standard Error of the Mean (SEM)?", "sigma / sqrt(n)", ["sigma * sqrt(n)", "sigma^2 / n", "s * n"], "SEM = sigma / sqrt(n), measuring the variability of sample means across repeated samples."),
        ("If variance of a dataset is 64, what is the standard deviation?", "8", ["16", "32", "4"], "Standard deviation is the square root of variance: sqrt(64) = 8."),
        ("What is the probability of drawing two Aces consecutively without replacement from a 52-card deck?", "(4/52) * (3/51) = 1/221", ["(4/52) * (4/52)", "1/13", "1/52"], "P(1st Ace) = 4/52; P(2nd Ace | 1st Ace) = 3/51. Product = 12 / 2652 = 1/221."),
        ("Two events A and B are mutually exclusive if:", "They cannot occur simultaneously (P(A and B) = 0)", ["P(A and B) = P(A)*P(B)", "P(A) = P(B)", "They are always independent"], "Mutually exclusive events cannot co-occur; their joint probability is zero."),
        ("Two events A and B are independent if:", "P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B)", ["P(A and B) = 0", "P(A) + P(B) = 1", "P(A) = 1 - P(B)"], "Independence means the occurrence of event A does not alter the probability of event B."),
