        ("What is Blind SQL Injection compared to standard SQL Injection?", "Database returns no direct error or data; attacker infers values via boolean true/false or time delays", ["Attacker cannot see computer screen", "Database is completely offline", "Query is encrypted"], "Blind SQLi uses conditional boolean statements (e.g., 1=1) or sleep delays (pg_sleep) to extract data character by character."),
        ("What is a Return-Oriented Programming (ROP) attack in binary exploitation?", "Attacker chains existing small machine instruction sequences ending in 'ret' (gadgets) to bypass DEP/NX", ["Overwriting heap metadata", "Compiling code from scratch", "Cracking passwords with GPU"], "ROP reuses existing executable code fragments already loaded in memory to achieve arbitrary execution despite DEP."),
        ("What is Heap Spraying in browser exploitation?", "Filling heap memory with target shellcode to increase probability of hitting it during jump", ["Cleaning computer RAM", "Defragmenting files", "Deleting browser cache"], "Heap spraying allocates thousands of uniform byte blocks containing NOP sleds and shellcode to exploit pointers."),
        ("What attack exploits differences between front-end reverse proxy and back-end server HTTP request parsing?", "HTTP Request Smuggling", ["SQL Injection", "Cross-Site Scripting", "Phishing"], "Request smuggling exploits discrepancies in Content-Length vs Transfer-Encoding headers to hijack pipelined requests."),
        ("What is DNSSEC designed to protect against in the domain name system?", "DNS cache poisoning and spoofing by cryptographically signing DNS resource records", ["DDoS attacks on root servers", "Domain registration fees", "Website defacement"], "DNSSEC uses digital signatures (RRSIG, DNSKEY) to guarantee DNS response origin authenticity and integrity."),
        ("In asymmetric cryptography, what problem does the Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange solve?", "Allows two parties to establish a shared secret key over an insecure unencrypted channel", ["Encrypts large files directly", "Generates digital certificates", "Signs PDF documents"], "Diffie-Hellman enables public exchange of generator powers, computing shared secret without transmitting key."),
        ("What does HSTS (HTTP Strict Transport Security) header enforce on browsers?", "Forces all future connections to the domain to strictly use HTTPS, refusing insecure HTTP fallbacks", ["Forces password changes", "Blocks cookies", "Enables VPN"], "'Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains' eliminates SSL-stripping MitM attacks."),
        ("What is the difference between an IDS (Intrusion Detection System) and an IPS (Intrusion Prevention System)?", "IDS alerts on malicious traffic passively; IPS actively blocks and drops malicious traffic inline", ["IDS is hardware; IPS is software", "IPS is deprecated", "IDS blocks traffic immediately"], "An IDS monitors mirrored traffic; an IPS sits inline on the network packet path to drop threat packets in real time."),
        ("What is the 'Rowhammer' hardware vulnerability in dynamic RAM (DRAM)?", "Repeatedly accessing memory rows leaks electrical charge, flipping bits in adjacent unaccessed rows", ["Hitting hard drives with hammers", "CPU overheating bug", "Overvolting power supply"], "Rowhammer is a physical hardware defect in dense DRAM where electrical leakage induces bit flips in adjacent cells."),
        ("What is Meltdown (CVE-2017-5754) CPU hardware vulnerability based on?", "Speculative execution out-of-order memory reads bypass kernel privilege boundaries via cache timing", ["Software buffer overflow", "Insecure password storage", "DDoS on microcode"], "Meltdown exploited speculative memory access in modern CPUs, leaking kernel memory to unprivileged user space."),
